From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 11:13:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09816/11362] Stop test subprocesses rewriting the tracked
 results record

Seven pytest runs on 2026-08-24 each stripped flagged_adversarial,
corrigendum_note and corrigendum_pending from files under results/.

Named the writers. test_experiment_1736_kanele_synth.py:14 shells out to
scripts/experiment_1736_kanele_synth.py, which rebuilds its artifact from a
hardcoded template, so every key the conductor stamped later is dropped. The
three archive/activate tests have the same shape in their *_script half.
The observed damage set is the subprocess-launching test set.

Root cause: tracked_results_guard works, and both its mechanisms are
per-interpreter. A PEP 578 audit hook belongs to the interpreter that added
it; a monkeypatched builtins.open belongs to that interpreter's memory.
Neither crosses fork+exec, so only child-process writes still land.

test_experiment_3361_* isolates this on one writer: the in-process half
leaves the tree clean, the subprocess half rewrites the committed artifact
and still reports green. The run's own mutation observer called the file
NOT ATTRIBUTED -- it could not see its own write. Silent non-firing.

Fix: child_results_guard wraps subprocess.Popen so each child Python starts
with a stdlib-only sitecustomize that reinstalls the redirect. Stdlib-only
matters -- the children run as bare /usr/bin/python with no carnot package.
Redirect, not refuse, matching the in-process layer; refusing is a call for
whoever owns REQ-REPORT-6157.

Overhead measured at +27.3 ms/spawn from importlib.find_spec's finder
protocol running at every child start; a plain isfile() scan took it to
-0.1 ms, below noise.

Listed in GUARD_TARGETS with its reason, because the qa-layer scope-check
hook refused the first commit for leaving a new guard unclassified.

Mutation marker e734921 deleted deliberately. It was armed by this session's
exp1736 diagnostic run, but every path it named was UNATTRIBUTED to that run
and none was under results/: three ops/.*_state.json files and
output/kanele_synth/post_synth.dcp, all concurrent-agent work. Those files
were left untouched rather than reverted. This session's own damage
(experiment_1736_kanele_synth.json, experiment_3361_archive_v309_activate_
v310.json) was restored with git checkout and verified byte-identical by md5
before this commit.

Does NOT catch: non-Python children, os.system/exec/posix_spawn, python -S
or -E, a child that overwrites PYTHONPATH, or anything outside results/.
The sixteen tests are still wrong to re-run production scripts against the
repo root; this makes the write harmless, not the test correct.

